92186517565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518084 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518084 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518084 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186517565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518084 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e68bf2f3-762f-5da1-9e8f-676a80dfc16c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518086 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518086 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518086 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518086 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186517614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518086 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ee82855a-a9a9-5945-b471-65f010904c24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518093 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518093 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518093 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518093 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518093 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518093 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518093 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518093 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186517799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518093 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f8f42b0d-1f1e-5535-971e-bc06dfe092ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518121 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518121 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2129" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518121 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518121 #edb/ref 1071 "$5\\cdot (-5)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518121 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b95da98-1f5f-5fa5-ad72-3959a1d46504" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518121 #edb/ref 1074 "We have a product of a positive number $(5)$ and a negative number $(-5).$ So the product will be negative:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot (-5)=-\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply $5$ by $5$ without any signs: $5\\cdot 5=25.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n5\\cdot (-5)=-25.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518125 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518125 #edb/ref 1069 "q-236322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518125 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518125 #edb/ref 1071 "The ratio $3:2$ is equivalent to the ratio{{field-1}}$:10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518125 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3851cb9c-32ab-50a5-b26d-1f3dac949677" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518125 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the ratio equivalent to $3:2$, we multiply both numbers by the same value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓\\times \\,\\,5 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓\\times \\,\\,5 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\boxed{} &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,10 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get $10$ from $2,$ we multiply $2$ by $5.$ So, to get $\\,\\boxed{}\\,$ from $3,$ we multiply $3$ by $5$ as well:\n\n$$\n3\\times 5=15\n$$\n\nThe ratio $3:2$ is equivalent to $15:10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518126 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518126 #edb/ref 1069 "q-350272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518126 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518126 #edb/ref 1071 "Ella and Nora run bakeries. In the previous week, $10%$ of Ella’s pastries were gluten-free, while the decimal proportion of Nora’s pastries that were gluten-free was $0.05.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Less than one fourth of Ella's pastries were gluten-free.\n\nII. More than one fourth of Nora's pastries were gluten-free.\n\nIII. Ella had a greater percentage of gluten-free pastries compared to Nora." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518126 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "38eccaa1-2de4-5013-b379-9718f328c233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518126 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n10%<\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 100%=25%,\n$$\n\nless than one-fourth of Ella's pastries were gluten-free.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n0.05<\\frac{1}{4}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, less than one-fourth of Nora's pastries were gluten-free.\n\n- Statement III is true. We can compare $10%$ and $0.05$ by converting the decimal into a percent.\n\nTo do this, we multiply it by $100:$\n\n$$\n0.05\\times 100=5%\n$$\n\nSince $10%>5%,$ Ella had a **greater** percentage of gluten-free pastries compared to Nora.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518127 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518127 #edb/ref 1069 "q-236832" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518127 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518127 #edb/ref 1071 "$6-43=${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518127 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "38fc7adf-0452-58e7-9840-3560c89df130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518127 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $43$ is larger than $6,$ the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem:\n\n$$\n43-6\n$$\n\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,3\\end{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,13\\end{aligned} \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  &  & \\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n43-6=37.\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n6-43=-37.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518169 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518169 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518169 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518169 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a3/a37bbb00bcf37dce19970f5494412eaa9c68e5db134354e3c9d790cd9dc7a5b5.png)\n\nWhat ratio describes the number of triangles to squares?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518169 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7fc0e3a0-04f8-58fa-8d72-05025f5c40e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518169 #edb/ref 1074 "We count the number of triangles. We see that there is $1$ triangle.\n\n We count the number of square. We see that there are $3$ squares.\n\nSo the ratio of triangles to squares is $\\frac{1}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518173 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518173 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98376" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518173 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518173 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\frac{7}{10}$ is a rational number\n\nII. $-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a rational number\n\nIII. $3$ is a rational number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518173 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c89a512-5fc0-599a-9173-8df36d4b9b39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518173 #edb/ref 1074 "A rational number is any number that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $\\frac{7}{10}$ is a rational number.\n\n- $-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a rational number. It can be written as $\\frac{(-5)}{3}.$\n\n- $3$ is a rational number. It can be written as $\\frac{3}{1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518175 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518175 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43872" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518175 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518175 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is **not** an algebraic expression?\n\nI. $4+2y$\n\nII. $x+\\sqrt{5}$\n\nIII. $7-4=3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518175 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8e57b39f-05a9-5f35-9efb-5ed20831848c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518175 #edb/ref 1074 "III is **not** an algebraic expression since it contains the equality sign." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518183 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518183 #edb/ref 1069 "q-171153" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518183 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518183 #edb/ref 1071 "$0÷(\\frac{2}{3})=${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518183 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a98d2b92-1f79-5299-a15c-071e314b9a2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518183 #edb/ref 1074 "Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore, $0÷(\\frac{2}{3})=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518187 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518187 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85435" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518187 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518187 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $26\\,489\\,225$\n\nII. $14\\,736\\,438$\n\nIII. $749\\,356\\,314$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518187 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd6001aa-5beb-57ae-855c-a9db7b063e64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518187 #edb/ref 1074 "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$\n\n- The number $26\\,489\\,225$ is not divisible by $2,$ so it is not divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- The number $14\\,736\\,438$ is divisible by $2,$ and it is also divisible by $3$ since the sum of its digits is divisible by $3:$\n\n$$\n1+4+7+3+6+4+3+8=36\n$$\n\nSo, $14\\,736\\,438$ is divisible by $6.$\n\n- The number $749\\,356\\,314$ is divisible by $2,$ and it is also divisible by $3$ since the sum of its digits is divisible by $3:$\n\n$$\n7+4+9+3+5+6+3+1+4=42\n$$\n\nSo, $749\\,356\\,314$ is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518189 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518189 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518189 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518189 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following shows the point $D(-5,4)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518189 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c49fa7b3-27b5-573b-aadb-233575243dcc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518189 #edb/ref 1074 "Starting at the origin, we move along the $x$ and $y$ axes to get to the point $D.$\n\nFirst, we move along the $x$-axis. The point $D$ has an $x$-coordinate of $-5,$ so we move $5$ units to the *left*, in the negative direction of the $x$-axis.\n\nThen, we move along the $y$-axis. The point $D$ has a $y$-coordinate of $4,$ so we move $4$ units *up*, in the positive direction of the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/19/19520b88504572a892e70dc3a40d16a3635c8528f9e591ac82aa0411d8091129.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518225 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518225 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "06956ae6-a0ed-51f5-858f-eee00b1ca616" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518225 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518228 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518228 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0e4a3401-c189-5f87-82f9-223689f4ee7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518228 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518232 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "27569124-56eb-5998-a405-7cf8748dd2b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518232 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518235 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518235 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "30e06609-47fd-5091-95a4-9a463054dd15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518235 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518242 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518242 #edb/ref 10